  "step_3": "The decision reason requires: (1) computing an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes (ignoring probabilities), which is -3000, and (2) evaluating which lottery has a higher probability of yielding an outcome above this level (i.e., positive difference). Outcomes above -3000 are only 0. So both the reason and the individual effectively compare the probability of getting 0. The reason would prefer B (55%) over A (10%)—same as the individual’s choice. However, the individual does not mention any aspiration level, nor do they reference deviations from a reference point or mean outcome. Instead, their reasoning is framed in terms of avoiding loss and minimizing the chance of losing money. This is a common heuristic (minimizing chance of loss), not necessarily rooted in aspiration-level comparisons. While the information used (probability of getting 0) overlaps with what the reason uses, the cognitive process—justification based on avoiding loss rather than exceeding an aspiration level—differs in rationale. There is no evidence the individual computed or referenced an aspiration level. Thus, while the same data (probability of zero) is used, the reasoning structure does not align with the reason’s rule. The match is superficial, not structural.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
